1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:12,285
Fellow pilots, most of us see real value in tracking aviation accidents.

2
00:00:12,335 --> 00:00:18,100
It's how we learn from what went wrong, to avoid it happening to us.

3
00:00:19,667 --> 00:00:26,663
We usually do that by following Blancolirio, COPA, Facebook groups and forums.

4
00:00:26,713 --> 00:00:29,338
That works, but it's passive:

5
00:00:29,388 --> 00:00:32,917
we catch whatever happens to scroll by.

6
00:00:34,467 --> 00:00:36,731
I wanted something active:

7
00:00:36,781 --> 00:00:43,517
to be pinged whenever a serious accident happens in a type like the one I fly.

8
00:00:45,067 --> 00:00:52,017
So, with help from my friend Claude, I built an app for exactly that:

9
00:00:52,067 --> 00:00:55,017
Tracking Piston GA Accidents.

10
00:00:56,567 --> 00:01:01,088
It's free, on the GeezerGeek Pilot Apps page.

11
00:01:01,138 --> 00:01:04,467
The link and a QR code are below.

12
00:01:07,533 --> 00:01:08,126
Up top:

13
00:01:08,176 --> 00:01:19,883
US piston airplane accidents since the first of October, fatal accidents, and whether fatals are running better or worse than the ten-year trend.

14
00:01:21,433 --> 00:01:25,538
Section one lists every one, newest first.

15
00:01:25,588 --> 00:01:29,016
Tap an accident for NTSB's details:

16
00:01:29,066 --> 00:01:35,783
injuries, damage, status, and the probable cause once it's published.

17
00:01:37,333 --> 00:01:41,422
NTSB often takes a week or more to list a case.

18
00:01:41,472 --> 00:01:51,783
When the FAA's next-day notice comes out, the accident shows here marked PRELIM, and the NTSB record replaces it later.

19
00:01:53,333 --> 00:01:59,283
You can narrow the list to your type, a whole brand, or all singles or twins.

20
00:02:00,833 --> 00:02:04,858
But the heart of it is section three, Alerts.

21
00:02:04,908 --> 00:02:07,083
This is the active part.

22
00:02:08,633 --> 00:02:10,536
Choose what pings you.

23
00:02:10,586 --> 00:02:13,847
Every fatal accident is on by default.

24
00:02:13,897 --> 00:02:20,483
Add other new accidents that match the severity and the types you care about.

25
00:02:22,033 --> 00:02:24,727
Enter your email, and Save.

26
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:30,708
Check your inbox for a short email with a confirm link.

27
00:02:30,758 --> 00:02:32,592
Click it, and you're set.

28
00:02:32,642 --> 00:02:36,750
You can send yourself a test ping to be sure it arrives.

29
00:02:38,300 --> 00:02:41,810
From then on, the news comes to you.

30
00:02:41,860 --> 00:02:47,391
A new fatal accident, from the FAA's notice or from NTSB.

31
00:02:47,441 --> 00:02:50,277
A new case in a type you fly.

32
00:02:50,327 --> 00:02:55,666
And when a case you're watching in section two changes:

33
00:02:55,716 --> 00:03:01,250
a preliminary report, a factual report, a probable cause.

34
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:09,900
Every ping has a one-click stop link, and nothing goes out until you confirm your address.

35
00:03:11,467 --> 00:03:18,045
Learning from accidents is one of the best safety habits we can develop as pilots.

36
00:03:18,095 --> 00:03:22,198
This app simply makes it active instead of passive.

37
00:03:22,248 --> 00:03:23,017
Fly safe.

